Organ donation saves thousands of lives every year.

17
00:01:07.454 --> 00:01:10.989
Your turn. Say: organ donation.

18
00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:14.957
organ donation.

19
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.357
patient autonomy.

20
00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:22.971
patient autonomy means a patient's right to make decisions about their own care.

21
00:01:23.271 --> 00:01:27.302
Patient autonomy means respecting a refusal of treatment.

22
00:01:28.002 --> 00:01:31.967
Your turn. Say: patient autonomy.

23
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.144
patient autonomy.

24
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.836
confidentiality.

25
00:01:39.536 --> 00:01:43.266
confidentiality means keeping private information secret.

26
00:01:43.566 --> 00:01:47.237
Medical confidentiality protects patients' trust.

27
00:01:47.937 --> 00:01:51.541
Your turn. Say: confidentiality.

28
00:01:54.241 --> 00:01:55.614
confidentiality.

29
00:01:56.617 --> 00:01:57.920
clinical ethics.

30
00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.709
clinical ethics means moral principles guiding medical decisions.

31
00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:05.659
The hospital has a clinical ethics committee.

32
00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:09.696
Your turn. Say: clinical ethics.

33
00:02:12.396 --> 00:02:13.885
clinical ethics.

34
00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:16.105
life support.

35
00:02:16.805 --> 00:02:20.952
life support means machines that keep a seriously ill person alive.

36
00:02:21.252 --> 00:02:23.751
The family had to decide about life support.

37
00:02:24.451 --> 00:02:27.452
Your turn. Say: life support.

38
00:02:30.152 --> 00:02:31.281
life support.

39
00:02:32.283 --> 00:02:33.528
genetic testing.

40
00:02:34.228 --> 00:02:39.119
genetic testing means analysing DNA to find health risks.

41
00:02:39.419 --> 00:02:42.417
Genetic testing can reveal inherited diseases.

42
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:46.175
Your turn. Say: genetic testing.

43
00:02:48.875 --> 00:02:50.225
genetic testing.

44
00:02:51.217 --> 00:02:53.518
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:53.818 --> 00:02:57.989
Rithy, imagine a patient refuses treatment that could save her life.

46
00:02:59.283 --> 00:03:02.606
Then the doctors must respect her autonomy, mustn't they?

47
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:07.042
Generally, yes, but they might have misunderstood her wishes.

48
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:13.533
True. She can't have given informed consent if nobody explained the risks clearly.

49
00:03:14.817 --> 00:03:20.351
Exactly. And what about genetic testing? Should employers ever see the results?

50
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.987
Absolutely not. That would break confidentiality.

51
00:03:26.283 --> 00:03:30.512
Some argue the family should be told if the result affects them too.

52
00:03:31.817 --> 00:03:35.360
That must be one of the hardest questions in clinical ethics.

53
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:40.066
Grammar focus: Modals of deduction (nuance).

54
00:03:40.366 --> 00:03:44.734
Modals of deduction show how certain you are, about the present or the past.

55
00:03:45.034 --> 00:03:50.795
Must and can't for strong certainty, might and could for possibility, and should for expectation.

56
00:03:51.095 --> 00:03:56.685
For example: She can't have understood the risks. The results should be ready by now.

57
00:03:56.985 --> 00:04:03.744
'Should' can express logical expectation, not only advice: The parcel should arrive today.

58
00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.611
Now read and listen. Who Decides?.

59
00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:11.559
Modern medicine can keep people alive longer than ever before.

60
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.974
Yet this power raises difficult questions about who should make decisions.

61
00:04:18.283 --> 00:04:22.535
Patient autonomy means that adults have the right to accept or refuse treatment.

62
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:28.090
However, decisions are only valid if they are based on informed consent.

63
00:04:29.383 --> 00:04:33.902
Life support creates further dilemmas when patients cannot express their wishes.

64
00:04:35.217 --> 00:04:40.061
Genetic testing adds another layer, since results may affect relatives as well.

65
00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:47.982
Hospital ethics committees must balance autonomy, confidentiality and the duty to protect life.

66
00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:53.860
There are rarely perfect answers, which is why open discussion is essential.

67
00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.791
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:58.091 --> 00:05:00.833
Keeping private information secret is blank.

69
00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:06.598
A, confidentiality. B, euthanasia. C, life support.

70
00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:13.767
The answer is A: confidentiality.

71
00:05:15.283 --> 00:05:18.644
He looks exhausted. He blank been working all night.

72
00:05:18.944 --> 00:05:22.795
A, can't have. B, must have. C, should.

73
00:05:26.783 --> 00:05:28.702
The answer is B: must have.

74
00:05:30.217 --> 00:05:33.528
She blank have received my message; her phone was off.

75
00:05:33.828 --> 00:05:36.982
A, must. B, can't. C, should.

76
00:05:40.983 --> 00:05:43.633
The answer is B: can't.

77
00:05:45.117 --> 00:05:48.509
A patient's right to make decisions about their care is blank.

78
00:05:48.809 --> 00:05:53.670
A, patient autonomy. B, organ donation. C, genetic testing.

79
00:05:57.683 --> 00:06:00.821
The answer is A: patient autonomy.

80
00:06:02.317 --> 00:06:06.870
According to the text, decisions are only valid if they are based on blank.

81
00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:13.274
A, family wishes. B, informed consent. C, doctors' opinions.

82
00:06:17.283 --> 00:06:20.223
The answer is B: informed consent.

83
00:06:21.717 --> 00:06:28.700
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should organ donation be automatic unless people choose to opt out?

84
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.391
Part two: Give your position, two arguments, and one possible problem.

85
00:06:44.691 --> 00:06:58.793
I support an opt-out system. Firstly, it would save many lives; secondly, most people say they agree with donation but never register. However, families might feel their wishes were ignored, so clear communication is essential.

86
00:06:59.093 --> 00:07:01.592
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:02.583 --> 00:07:08.301
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modals of deduction (nuance).

88
00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:12.902
Today's value is empathy. Think about how others feel.

89
00:07:13.202 --> 00:07:15.854
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:16.154 --> 00:07:18.888
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

